{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1303 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "5df3284e5b5d1e8f5e3d68f4b890b944506cd45ec4129ed86922148e1c8e895b" :edb/db-before-t 1302 :edb/db-after-t 1303 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534615 50 #inst "2026-10-08T02:33:37.724Z" 13194139534615 true] [17592186096346 1100 "Consider the function $y = f(x)$ below, defined on $x\\in(-2,8).$ At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/54d2fe17d8d36a23c7c714242ffbfa12a56fd8e870b7672f1ce9b7e02f6508b4.png)" 13194139534615 true] [17592186096346 1100 "Consider the function $y = f(x)$ below, defined on $x\\in(-2,8).$ At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/54d2fe17d8d36a23c7c714242ffbfa12a56fd8e870b7672f1ce9b7e02f6508b4.png)" 13194139534615 false] [17592186096347 1100 "The function $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y = f''(x)$ is shown below. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/6b146f4a7bec69f1e984b3e2aa0e3c38ea65c4464da037759b41eac2fd97325f.png)" 13194139534615 true] [17592186096347 1100 "The function $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y = f''(x)$ is shown below. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/6b146f4a7bec69f1e984b3e2aa0e3c38ea65c4464da037759b41eac2fd97325f.png)" 13194139534615 false] [17592186096544 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298902 13194139534615 true] [17592186096544 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298923 13194139534615 true] [17592186096544 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298970 13194139534615 true] [17592186096544 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298977 13194139534615 true] [17592186096545 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298895 13194139534615 true] [17592186096545 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298916 13194139534615 true] [17592186096545 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298984 13194139534615 true] [17592186096546 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298930 13194139534615 true] [17592186096546 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298937 13194139534615 true] [17592186096546 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298944 13194139534615 true] [17592186096546 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298951 13194139534615 true] [17592186096547 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298909 13194139534615 true] [17592186096547 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298965 13194139534615 true] [17592186097104 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534615 true] [17592186097104 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nFind the $x$-coordinate of all points of inflection for $g(x) = x^{4} - 4x^{3} + 6x^{2} - 2x$." 13194139534615 false] [17592186097104 1100 "Find the $x$-coordinate of all points of inflection for $g(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-2x.$" 13194139534615 true] [17592186097104 1145 "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}+12x-2 \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & =12{x}^{2}-24x+12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{2}-24x+12 & =0 \\\\ 12(x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=1.$ So we pick two points around it, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=0\\,$ and $\\,x=2,$ \n\nand compute the sign of ${g}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(0) & =12(0-1)^{2}=12>0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(2) & =12(2-1)^{2}=12>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=1,$ we conclude that $x=1$ is *not* a point of inflection." 13194139534615 true] [17592186097137 1017 #edb/ref 17592186095184 13194139534615 false] [17592186097137 1017 #edb/ref 17592186298958 13194139534615 true] [17592186097137 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534615 true] [17592186097137 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/19e9763d62ca7818024bf49522a127a48dd04f76d1175c93e060a1043b6a5795.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" 13194139534615 true] [17592186097137 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1046/Source/Images/q-31213.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x$. The graph $y = f^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" 13194139534615 false] [17592186097137 1145 "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch sign. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=0$ and $x=4.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=0$ and $x=4.$" 13194139534615 true] [17592186097238 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/a8fadcc272d96492194b2d46f1ae66151fb4fd16b2ee42a6840ff335aa0b4262.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. What are the points of inflection?" 13194139534615 true] [17592186097238 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/a8fadcc272d96492194b2d46f1ae66151fb4fd16b2ee42a6840ff335aa0b4262.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. What are the points of inflection?" 13194139534615 false] [17592186294015 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/5d776a61d1039639d91e2c667c68965d3e2c901cdc926e8c54e6356cf1d87c2e.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $g(x)$ have a point of inflection?" 13194139534615 true] [17592186294015 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/5d776a61d1039639d91e2c667c68965d3e2c901cdc926e8c54e6356cf1d87c2e.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $g(x)$ have a point of inflection?" 13194139534615 false] [17592186294039 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298964 13194139534615 true] [17592186294054 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298969 13194139534615 true] [17592186298895 1017 #edb/ref 17592186298896 13194139534615 true] [17592186298895 1089 "q-114402" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298895 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534615 true] [17592186298895 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/20b6532fc5d99c74a26bd752e19aa2a6451ea26a79d2a129e4c0203bc9133354.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x$ in $(-4,4).$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298895 1113 #uuid "444031a3-3309-59e7-a714-c81365022d27" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298895 1145 "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=-2,$ $x=1,$ and $x=3.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-2,$ $x=1,$ and $x=3.$" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298896 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298897 13194139534615 true] [17592186298896 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298898 13194139534615 true] [17592186298896 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298899 13194139534615 true] [17592186298896 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298900 13194139534615 true] [17592186298896 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298901 13194139534615 true] [17592186298896 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534615 true] [17592186298896 1041 "selection" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298896 1104 #edb/ref 17592186298900 13194139534615 true] [17592186298896 1110 #uuid "a45ea804-c24e-5861-a32d-d80d7817241b" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298897 1034 "$x=-2$ and $x=1$ only" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298897 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298897 1111 #uuid "41aa9c73-5af6-5b30-be41-696e1204b901" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298898 1034 "$x=0$ only" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298898 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298898 1111 #uuid "d1bdf0f5-b3fe-5471-a098-cea3d66ac947" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298899 1034 "$x=1$ and $x=3$ only" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298899 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298899 1111 #uuid "f7f384a9-2f74-52c8-882c-570343ba1435" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298900 1034 "$x=-2,$ $x=1,$ and $x=3$" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298900 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298900 1111 #uuid "4b780da8-cbf8-5cb3-b18c-a90e9b11bf9f" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298901 1034 "$x=-2$ and $x=3$ only" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298901 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298901 1111 #uuid "c7ade8a1-544b-55c3-aba7-3741f93d9a33" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298902 1017 #edb/ref 17592186298903 13194139534615 true] [17592186298902 1089 "q-114404" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298902 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534615 true] [17592186298902 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/0c9e74029cf6761469c72dc76dc6b13ba92107857c2a2d63ca6983dec282db37.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. What are the points of inflection?" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298902 1113 #uuid "61ca6f40-387d-5020-96de-c8055fd09d7c" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298902 1145 "The points of inflection are the points where\n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph.\n\n- The point $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection. The function does not change concavity. It's concave down both to the left and to the right of $x=0.$\n\n- The point $x=1$ is *not* a point of inflection. Although the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=1,$  the function is not defined at this point, so the tangent line does not exist either.\n\n- The point $x=2$ is not a point of inflection. The function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, there are no points of inflection." 13194139534615 true] [17592186298903 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298904 13194139534615 true] [17592186298903 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298905 13194139534615 true] [17592186298903 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298906 13194139534615 true] [17592186298903 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298907 13194139534615 true] [17592186298903 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298908 13194139534615 true] [17592186298903 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534615 true] [17592186298903 1041 "selection" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298903 1104 #edb/ref 17592186298906 13194139534615 true] [17592186298903 1110 #uuid "1e0b0df6-26c0-5280-9e76-20c7d65d1bd7" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298904 1034 "$x=2$ only" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298904 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298904 1111 #uuid "14c5f5e2-1cb0-5541-a8b5-2ef8cfe8fedf" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298905 1034 "$x=0$ only" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298905 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298905 1111 #uuid "222934c1-158c-5d7c-bed8-961584e11904" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298906 1034 "There are no points of inflection" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298906 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298906 1111 #uuid "b540a420-c4e6-51d4-86d2-bcac80d042f0" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298907 1034 "$x=1$ and $x=2$ only" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298907 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298907 1111 #uuid "51526fb8-c340-5715-8138-057213541ef9" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298908 1034 "$x=0,$ $x=1,$ and $x=2$" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298908 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298908 1111 #uuid "fe0a9a2f-073f-5bbc-b545-28af755c893b" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298909 1017 #edb/ref 17592186298910 13194139534615 true] [17592186298909 1089 "q-114413" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298909 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534615 true] [17592186298909 1100 "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-4x+7).$" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298909 1113 #uuid "5145ec8c-5097-582c-b02c-15f0c24181cd" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298909 1145 "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-4x+7)+{e}^{x}(2x-4) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-2x+3) \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-2x+3)+{e}^{x}(2x-2) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}({x}^{2}+1)>0$ for all real values of $x,$ the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0$ has no real solutions, and so there are no points of inflection." 13194139534615 true] [17592186298910 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298911 13194139534615 true] [17592186298910 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298912 13194139534615 true] [17592186298910 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298913 13194139534615 true] [17592186298910 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298914 13194139534615 true] [17592186298910 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298915 13194139534615 true] [17592186298910 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534615 true] [17592186298910 1041 "selection" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298910 1104 #edb/ref 17592186298915 13194139534615 true] [17592186298910 1110 #uuid "5efe734d-5228-5332-aacd-08464c673489" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298911 1034 "x=-1" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298911 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534615 true] [17592186298911 1111 #uuid "0f8dcdd9-07a4-5751-b163-141ec66cb107" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298912 1034 "x=1" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298912 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534615 true] [17592186298912 1111 #uuid "f35f1df9-77b9-5fe4-bb1a-511f3b9d2549" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298913 1034 "x=\\sqrt{2}" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298913 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534615 true] [17592186298913 1111 #uuid "1100cc7f-aaa6-55ff-b1c7-2017629861cf" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298914 1034 "x=0" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298914 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534615 true] [17592186298914 1111 #uuid "7b123f5f-7687-556d-bb81-fd29f6f16416" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298915 1034 "There are no points of inflection" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298915 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298915 1111 #uuid "4662a91e-0f0c-514d-ab7d-d9d9d14d1bdd" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298916 1017 #edb/ref 17592186298917 13194139534615 true] [17592186298916 1089 "q-114416" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298916 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534615 true] [17592186298916 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/826336bca267300c9b98e43072f74bb5efdacff0a4007fb7b938eb61893d8fbe.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x$ in $(-4,4).$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298916 1113 #uuid "c94ce91c-0982-58bc-9de3-2473b47fabe5" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298916 1145 "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=2.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has point of inflection at $x=2$ only." 13194139534615 true] [17592186298917 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298918 13194139534615 true] [17592186298917 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298919 13194139534615 true] [17592186298917 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298920 13194139534615 true] [17592186298917 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298921 13194139534615 true] [17592186298917 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298922 13194139534615 true] [17592186298917 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534615 true] [17592186298917 1041 "selection" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298917 1104 #edb/ref 17592186298919 13194139534615 true] [17592186298917 1110 #uuid "4ae4f4c4-6699-5a4a-8667-b6e7ae1f07d9" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298918 1034 "x=\\pm 2" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298918 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534615 true] [17592186298918 1111 #uuid "6e7456b2-074f-5fe7-8eb4-44865d674201" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298919 1034 "$x=2$ only" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298919 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298919 1111 #uuid "91e3d238-3616-50fa-861b-91e6c1dcebd7" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298920 1034 "x=\\pm 1" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298920 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534615 true] [17592186298920 1111 #uuid "71a97db9-7012-5fa0-9cf5-70ef868887a4" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298921 1034 "$f(x)$ has no inflection points" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298921 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298921 1111 #uuid "169abb35-6522-5029-ab94-aaf688833eb2" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298922 1034 "$x=-2$ only" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298922 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298922 1111 #uuid "d71d9e2a-d68e-56b2-b7c6-7b4a6d009d2b" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298923 1017 #edb/ref 17592186298924 13194139534615 true] [17592186298923 1089 "q-127112" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298923 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534615 true] [17592186298923 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/885f9b9ea95909edf81c756397bff73ca8a83619117f51c9db7433c5082cf354.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=h(x)$ above. What is the point of inflection?" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298923 1113 #uuid "19c35970-5e9e-5ce0-9a18-4aeadc55467f" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298923 1145 "The points of inflection are the points where\n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph.\n\n- The point $x=-1$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=-1,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\n- The point $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=0.$ Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\n- The point $x=1$ is *not* a point of inflection. Although the graph changes concavity from concave up to concave down at $x=1,$   the function is not defined at this point, so the tangent line does not exist either.\n\nTherefore, the point of inflection is $x=-1$ only." 13194139534615 true] [17592186298924 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298925 13194139534615 true] [17592186298924 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298926 13194139534615 true] [17592186298924 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298927 13194139534615 true] [17592186298924 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298928 13194139534615 true] [17592186298924 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298929 13194139534615 true] [17592186298924 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534615 true] [17592186298924 1041 "selection" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298924 1104 #edb/ref 17592186298927 13194139534615 true] [17592186298924 1110 #uuid "278ad05f-436c-5367-87c0-9d7cb55a4dcb" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298925 1034 "$x=-1$ and $x=0$" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298925 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298925 1111 #uuid "dbda536a-a30e-52c7-884c-a13f44e27330" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298926 1034 "$x=1$ only" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298926 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298926 1111 #uuid "746a2a7e-fc82-541e-8977-6ce74600fb89" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298927 1034 "$x=-1$ only" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298927 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298927 1111 #uuid "3be0c5ab-7caf-5603-aed2-ea5f3e51ee1c" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298928 1034 "$x=-1$ and $x=1$" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298928 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298928 1111 #uuid "29f51c33-ab49-51a4-a3e7-528fc07c38b7" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298929 1034 "$x=0$ and $x=1$" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298929 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298929 1111 #uuid "ac8b9b7e-24b0-5f32-a79b-8c280d688d16" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298930 1017 #edb/ref 17592186298931 13194139534615 true] [17592186298930 1089 "q-21047" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298930 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534615 true] [17592186298930 1100 "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=8{x}^{2}-3x-\\frac{1}{x}.$" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298930 1113 #uuid "43a049b9-92e8-5818-8daf-281bf457ff15" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298930 1145 "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, notice that $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=0,$ so this can't be a point of inflection.\n\nNow, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =16x-3+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =16-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & =16 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ and $x=1$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{4}) & =16-\\frac{2}{{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3}}=-112<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =16-\\frac{2}{(1)^{3}}=14>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we conclude that $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ is indeed a point of inflection." 13194139534615 true] [17592186298931 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298932 13194139534615 true] [17592186298931 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298933 13194139534615 true] [17592186298931 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298934 13194139534615 true] [17592186298931 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298935 13194139534615 true] [17592186298931 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298936 13194139534615 true] [17592186298931 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534615 true] [17592186298931 1041 "selection" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298931 1104 #edb/ref 17592186298932 13194139534615 true] [17592186298931 1110 #uuid "898e8b2c-817b-5b6d-b909-be519e9eb032" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298932 1034 "x=\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298932 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534615 true] [17592186298932 1111 #uuid "fba25312-794c-546b-8978-1447f36282ec" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298933 1034 "x=4" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298933 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534615 true] [17592186298933 1111 #uuid "a9205c82-6fd0-5c53-95e6-38718a4f2164" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298934 1034 "x=-\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298934 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534615 true] [17592186298934 1111 #uuid "79fc881a-1905-50fa-a4e8-3d4d0c9c011e" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298935 1034 "x=-4" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298935 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534615 true] [17592186298935 1111 #uuid "ec7c21d3-47fc-59b0-9678-4719bc0e838e" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298936 1034 "x=-2" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298936 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534615 true] [17592186298936 1111 #uuid "ee95f8d3-6d03-5a52-9899-381aeba3d399" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298937 1017 #edb/ref 17592186298938 13194139534615 true] [17592186298937 1089 "q-2460" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298937 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534615 true] [17592186298937 1100 "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)={x}^{3}.$" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298937 1113 #uuid "137cf379-31e0-5aef-a23a-29d963a5bad4" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298937 1145 "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n6x=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=0.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=-1$ and $x=1$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1) & =6(-1)=-6<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =6(1)=6>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Therefore, since the concavity changes at $x=0,$ we conclude that $x=0$ is indeed a point of inflection." 13194139534615 true] [17592186298938 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298939 13194139534615 true] [17592186298938 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298940 13194139534615 true] [17592186298938 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298941 13194139534615 true] [17592186298938 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298942 13194139534615 true] [17592186298938 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298943 13194139534615 true] [17592186298938 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534615 true] [17592186298938 1041 "selection" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298938 1104 #edb/ref 17592186298942 13194139534615 true] [17592186298938 1110 #uuid "4be8ad13-3a15-5bec-88b9-a5aa40d7a154" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298939 1034 "x=-6" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298939 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534615 true] [17592186298939 1111 #uuid "ca8858be-4fe4-5ebf-be03-cb245cef263a" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298940 1034 "x=6" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298940 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534615 true] [17592186298940 1111 #uuid "477f16b0-d9d3-5bc6-aa65-b18f291ff324" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298941 1034 "x=2" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298941 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534615 true] [17592186298941 1111 #uuid "12d78119-1241-5d3b-9cea-1301412362fd" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298942 1034 "x=0" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298942 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534615 true] [17592186298942 1111 #uuid "5a4b95a5-b48b-50f9-a4de-87c5be2b29ba" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298943 1034 "No inflection point exists" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298943 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298943 1111 #uuid "fbba851c-96e0-5a8a-a6a2-7cc331323305" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298944 1017 #edb/ref 17592186298945 13194139534615 true] [17592186298944 1089 "q-2465" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298944 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534615 true] [17592186298944 1100 "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-6x+7.$" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298944 1113 #uuid "9b979a9b-1d53-5944-a4fd-b2809780da32" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298944 1145 "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}+12x-6 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =12x+12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n12x+12=0\\,⟹\\,x=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=-1.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=-2$ and $x=0$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2) & =12(-2)+12=-12<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =12(0)+12=12>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-1,$ we conclude that $x=-1$ is indeed a point of inflection." 13194139534615 true] [17592186298945 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298946 13194139534615 true] [17592186298945 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298947 13194139534615 true] [17592186298945 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298948 13194139534615 true] [17592186298945 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298949 13194139534615 true] [17592186298945 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298950 13194139534615 true] [17592186298945 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534615 true] [17592186298945 1041 "selection" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298945 1104 #edb/ref 17592186298946 13194139534615 true] [17592186298945 1110 #uuid "579774fb-b006-5be8-ae5a-a8c08dc7c61e" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298946 1034 "x=-1" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298946 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534615 true] [17592186298946 1111 #uuid "51bea530-f60f-5ca5-bf53-fb7d5fad6712" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298947 1034 "x=1" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298947 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534615 true] [17592186298947 1111 #uuid "f21924f5-058e-506d-be3b-867e8b61f62e" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298948 1034 "x=2" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298948 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534615 true] [17592186298948 1111 #uuid "afb139f0-4170-5a14-84dc-cbb263ad15aa" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298949 1034 "x=0" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298949 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534615 true] [17592186298949 1111 #uuid "95fef0a5-88ac-5c37-bfda-b6906fff3987" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298950 1034 "x=-2" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298950 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534615 true] [17592186298950 1111 #uuid "2ac1fe55-14d6-5ec0-ae84-365d41915a00" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298951 1017 #edb/ref 17592186298952 13194139534615 true] [17592186298951 1089 "q-2753" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298951 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534615 true] [17592186298951 1100 "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ on the interval $(0,2\\pi ).$" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298951 1113 #uuid "82843c7f-f0f2-5349-b0a0-01241922fac4" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298951 1145 "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0$ on the interval $(0,2\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pi \n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=\\pi .$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=\\pi ,$ we conclude that $x=\\pi $ is indeed a point of inflection." 13194139534615 true] [17592186298952 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298953 13194139534615 true] [17592186298952 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298954 13194139534615 true] [17592186298952 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298955 13194139534615 true] [17592186298952 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298956 13194139534615 true] [17592186298952 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298957 13194139534615 true] [17592186298952 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534615 true] [17592186298952 1041 "selection" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298952 1104 #edb/ref 17592186298955 13194139534615 true] [17592186298952 1110 #uuid "30be49a0-1ad1-529c-833f-cacee5950bd1" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298953 1034 "x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298953 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534615 true] [17592186298953 1111 #uuid "78c103ee-6059-5303-a35f-46554447d0e4" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298954 1034 "There are no points of inflection" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298954 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298954 1111 #uuid "3a1ed625-4341-58b3-aa10-33f47944f557" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298955 1034 "x=\\pi " 13194139534615 true] [17592186298955 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534615 true] [17592186298955 1111 #uuid "d5edd98b-8127-5e02-855e-df6e05d9c4c5" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298956 1034 "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298956 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534615 true] [17592186298956 1111 #uuid "34e09784-599c-5cd4-bb0e-ff216bc290e6" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298957 1034 "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298957 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534615 true] [17592186298957 1111 #uuid "383c080f-9929-598b-93c7-36281679d275" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298958 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298959 13194139534615 true] [17592186298958 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298960 13194139534615 true] [17592186298958 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298961 13194139534615 true] [17592186298958 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298962 13194139534615 true] [17592186298958 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298963 13194139534615 true] [17592186298958 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534615 true] [17592186298958 1041 "selection" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298958 1104 #edb/ref 17592186298960 13194139534615 true] [17592186298958 1110 #uuid "236ebe4f-089a-5ef0-a6cc-de2d76cf4d01" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298959 1034 "$x=-4$ and $x=4$" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298959 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298959 1111 #uuid "decc4370-a24d-5c18-9598-95f621a07e51" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298960 1034 "$x=0$ and $x=4$" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298960 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298960 1111 #uuid "636e595b-851f-5d2c-9658-825bedb3b34c" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298961 1034 "$x=-6$ and $x=-2$" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298961 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298961 1111 #uuid "55705a75-81a9-594a-8ad8-92b37266c1f4" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298962 1034 "x=0" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298962 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534615 true] [17592186298962 1111 #uuid "ed0b3677-de6c-5e44-a12e-eef28d35cebf" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298963 1034 "$x=-4,$ $x=0,$ and $x=4$" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298963 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298963 1111 #uuid "99a44cc3-3c65-5a9f-abeb-d3b5530603e0" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298964 1034 "2" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298964 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534615 true] [17592186298964 1111 #uuid "88841588-0fb1-5108-9559-255db6413849" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298965 1017 #edb/ref 17592186298966 13194139534615 true] [17592186298965 1089 "q-344206" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298965 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534615 true] [17592186298965 1100 "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection for $g(x)=3{x}^{5}+15{x}^{4}-2x+5.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298965 1113 #uuid "9830b87c-ec53-5f59-89c6-b1659da4f4ec" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298965 1145 "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${g}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =15{x}^{4}+60{x}^{3}-2 \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & =60{x}^{3}+180{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60{x}^{3}+180{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 60{x}^{2}(x+3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-3$ and $x=0.$ So we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=-4,\\,$ $x=-1,\\,$ and $x=1,$\n\n and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(-4) & =60(-4)^{3}+180(-4)^{2}=-960<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(-1) & =60(-1)^{3}+180(-1)^{2}=120>0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(1) & =60(1)^{3}+180(1)^{2}=240>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=-3.$ Therefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-3,$ we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed a point of inflection.\n\n- The second derivative does not change sign at $x=0.$ Therefore, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=0,$ we conclude that $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection.\n\nTherefore, only  $x=-3$ is a point of inflection." 13194139534615 true] [17592186298966 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298967 13194139534615 true] [17592186298966 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298968 13194139534615 true] [17592186298966 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534615 true] [17592186298966 1041 "field-1" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298966 1104 #edb/ref 17592186298967 13194139534615 true] [17592186298966 1110 #uuid "86e3b102-6275-5f93-ab85-bc58a01f6d2d" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298967 1034 "-3" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298967 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534615 true] [17592186298967 1111 #uuid "2992ee29-95d1-58dc-a11b-42f2c03ce2c8" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298968 1034 "0" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298968 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534615 true] [17592186298968 1111 #uuid "fd655f84-7eed-549b-8f62-60b2c9c27764" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298969 1034 "0" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298969 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534615 true] [17592186298969 1111 #uuid "722f578c-86db-52bc-9c57-5e83bbf92be2" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298970 1017 #edb/ref 17592186298971 13194139534615 true] [17592186298970 1089 "q-51253" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298970 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534615 true] [17592186298970 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/7d71b69f27d14ca6c9ff0467ecce1df7be2f53684b162a316dc8877218b202ca.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. What are the points of inflection?" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298970 1113 #uuid "3a15760c-3531-560b-9465-64e8a627b33c" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298970 1145 "The points of inflection are the points where\n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph.\n\n- The point $x=-2$ is not a point of inflection. Although the graph changes concavity from concave up to concave down at $x=-2,$   the function is not defined at this point, so the tangent line does not exist either.\n\n- The point $x=0$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=0,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point\n\n- The point $x=2$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=2.$ Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\n- The point $x=3$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=3.$ Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\nTherefore, the point of inflection is $x=0$ only." 13194139534615 true] [17592186298971 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298972 13194139534615 true] [17592186298971 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298973 13194139534615 true] [17592186298971 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298974 13194139534615 true] [17592186298971 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298975 13194139534615 true] [17592186298971 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298976 13194139534615 true] [17592186298971 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534615 true] [17592186298971 1041 "selection" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298971 1104 #edb/ref 17592186298972 13194139534615 true] [17592186298971 1110 #uuid "dbe501a8-801b-5b97-9881-09e4fc4d22d3" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298972 1034 "$x=0$ only" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298972 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298972 1111 #uuid "0013fc75-105f-51bf-b236-1b455c694d4f" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298973 1034 "$x=0$, $x=2$ and $x=3$" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298973 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298973 1111 #uuid "954c1556-51b7-5e5c-a75d-185d2ae85f6c" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298974 1034 "$x=0$, $x=\\pm 2$ and $x=3$" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298974 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298974 1111 #uuid "c385bbb3-ad7d-5d2b-a5bd-1e1fe190b479" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298975 1034 "$x=-2$ and $x=0$" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298975 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298975 1111 #uuid "7683b4df-c3ac-56a9-a427-0f00ca981f4c" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298976 1034 "$x=0$ and $x=3$" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298976 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298976 1111 #uuid "cf6e48be-e7ce-5aa7-ae12-adb794b1f3d5" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298977 1017 #edb/ref 17592186298978 13194139534615 true] [17592186298977 1089 "q-51423" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298977 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534615 true] [17592186298977 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/c19b9819f3fa99aafcbc88e1b020bef760891bcffdf3a8ae7b1ed50dd657655c.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. What are the points of inflection?" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298977 1113 #uuid "8d4e35db-d1ef-591b-b705-c5eebc3ef913" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298977 1145 "The points of inflection are the points where\n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph.\n\n- The point $x=0$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=0,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\n- The point $x=1$  is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=1.$ Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\n- The point $x=2$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=2.$ Second, the function is not defined at this point, so the tangent line does not exist either.\n\nTherefore, the point of inflection is $x=0$ only." 13194139534615 true] [17592186298978 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298979 13194139534615 true] [17592186298978 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298980 13194139534615 true] [17592186298978 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298981 13194139534615 true] [17592186298978 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298982 13194139534615 true] [17592186298978 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298983 13194139534615 true] [17592186298978 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534615 true] [17592186298978 1041 "selection" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298978 1104 #edb/ref 17592186298981 13194139534615 true] [17592186298978 1110 #uuid "d2cac989-c353-5a27-ab6f-0157deef9472" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298979 1034 "$x=0$ and $x=2$" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298979 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298979 1111 #uuid "f15cb921-e01e-5c44-b92c-65e3aa1dc732" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298980 1034 "$x=1$ and $x=2$" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298980 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298980 1111 #uuid "546e66be-e783-5646-9a01-c528d19bdafd" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298981 1034 "$x=0$ only" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298981 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298981 1111 #uuid "b0da4bd6-15f6-5ded-ad62-6714c3255db7" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298982 1034 "$x=1$ only" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298982 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298982 1111 #uuid "eb45b26b-5768-57de-97fe-6add6959626a" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298983 1034 "$x=0$ and $x=1$" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298983 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298983 1111 #uuid "bec13d20-7547-5fcb-a8a2-62591c09858b" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298984 1017 #edb/ref 17592186298985 13194139534615 true] [17592186298984 1089 "q-79199" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298984 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534615 true] [17592186298984 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/dba173a7cf462e7e67067a78c12ea05a50ea5713da0e6ddaed152055b3ce589f.png)\n\nThe function $h(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={h}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $h(x)$ have a point of inflection?" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298984 1113 #uuid "cf542050-9ffb-55f9-a8d4-4542e17194b9" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298984 1145 "First, notice that since $h(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${h}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${h}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={h}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at  $x=-2,$  $x=0,$ and $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-2,$ $x=0,$ and $x=3.$" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298985 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298986 13194139534615 true] [17592186298985 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298987 13194139534615 true] [17592186298985 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298988 13194139534615 true] [17592186298985 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298989 13194139534615 true] [17592186298985 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298990 13194139534615 true] [17592186298985 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534615 true] [17592186298985 1041 "selection" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298985 1104 #edb/ref 17592186298990 13194139534615 true] [17592186298985 1110 #uuid "10d62fd4-a3a3-59db-bf3c-405ceb1d6f48" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298986 1034 "x=3" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298986 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534615 true] [17592186298986 1111 #uuid "11afb0da-5f86-5fbf-9002-6b4185d8b7cd" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298987 1034 "$x=-3$ and $x=2$" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298987 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298987 1111 #uuid "a133126a-3a03-5f98-a4f9-aecfd33bec13" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298988 1034 "x=0" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298988 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534615 true] [17592186298988 1111 #uuid "a6d6c918-da41-51bc-a297-cccc3e1df1eb" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298989 1034 "$x=-2$ and $x=3$" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298989 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534615 true] [17592186298989 1111 #uuid "d3c8d33e-02d7-5634-9e94-8b53e23658dc" 13194139534615 true] [17592186298990 1034 "$x=-2,$ $x=0,$ and $x=3$